# What tests/ve/run.sh has ve do.
#
# "=file NAME" begins a file.  "== NAME" begins a check: the lines to
# "--" are given to sh in an empty directory that has a copy of each
# file, and must write exactly the lines after it.  There
#
#	k KEYS [ARG ...]	runs ve with the arguments and types KEYS
#	s KEYS [ARG ...]	the same, and then shows the screen
#
# KEYS are as printf has them: \033 is ESC, \r Return, \t a tab, and a %
# or a \ is written twice.  An ESC must not have [ or O straight after
# it, which is how the keys with arrows begin: \003, the interrupt, ends
# an insertion as well.  The screen is 24 by 80 unless VEROWS and VECOLS
# say otherwise; what stands out on it is between { and }.
